# A song's form: phrases and sections named with letters by their tune, not their words; verse and
# chorus in Jingle Bells, Clementine's chorus on its verse's tune, ternary A B A, and the rondo,
# A B A C A, in Beethoven's Für Elise, with the old city's street band and its clock tower.
lesson music-song-form v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=7 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A song's form"
  goal "Name the form of a piece with letters that follow its tunes, not its words."

  look {
    scene 36x16 {
      row top space=2 align=end at=canvas(1, 1) {
        citywalls walls towers=3 open=1
        table plan cols=2 head=["Jingle Bells", "Für Elise"] cells=["verse", "A", "chorus", "B", "verse", "A", "chorus", "C", "verse", "A"]
      }
    }
    say """
      In the old city a street band plays by the gate. Every piece they play has a plan, called its
      form, and each part gets a letter by its tune: the same tune gets the same letter, whatever
      the words. Jingle Bells has verses and a chorus. Each verse has new words to one tune, A, and
      the chorus has a tune of its own, B, so the song goes A B A B. Clementine's chorus is sung to
      its verse's tune, so all of Clementine is A. A piece that goes A B A, out to a new tune and
      home again, is in ternary form. Beethoven's Für Elise is a rondo: its first tune, A, keeps
      coming back, with a different new tune, B and then C, between the returns.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice music.form-letters count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    level easy {
      worked music.form-chorus-count q=1 level=medium
    }
    practice music.form-chorus-count count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice music.form-rondo-which count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    show music.form-fur-elise
    practice music.form-ternary count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    show music.form-letters f=1 hard-f=3
    show music.form-tap-chorus
    level hard {
      show music.form-claim q=1 r=0
    }
    practice music.oldcity-chime-notes count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.form-claim q=0 r=0 easy-r=1 hard-r=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.form-plan-count v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "A form's letters follow the tune, not the words: the same tune always gets the same letter."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a piece has a plan made of sections, that we name the plan with letters by
    giving the same tune the same letter, and that verse and chorus with two tunes (A B A B),
    ternary form (A B A) and the rondo (A B A C A) are different plans. The wrong ideas to watch for
    are giving a section a new letter because its words are new, when its tune is the same
    (Clementine's chorus is sung to its verse's tune, so the whole song is A), and calling any piece
    where something returns a rondo. In the working, look for the child comparing a section with the
    first one before giving it a letter. The core asks for a reason once, whether a plan with a
    return is a rondo; the two-star question tests Clementine against the rondo's rule; the
    three-star question counts every plan that starts and ends with A, which is easiest by filling
    the middle sections one at a time. A child who is stuck can sing a song they know and hold up a
    hand at every new section, then name the sections, and go back to the way in; stay on this
    lesson another day if verses still get new letters. Playing and tapping are judged on the music
    page; on paper you are the judge. Off the page, listen to Für Elise together and raise a hand
    each time A comes back.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the forms to name are A A B B and A B A B, the fourth chorus of Clementine is found once in the teacher's pen before the choruses are counted, the rondo is picked from a short list, the plan to judge is A B A, which is ternary, the chimes are counted over one hour ending on the hour, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the three-star plans a piece of three sections."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the forms to name are A A B A and A B A C, the song is counted in bars, the rondo is picked from forms that nearly are one, the plan to judge is a long rondo, A B A C A D A, a claim about Für Elise's sections is judged before the two-star, the chimes are counted over times that cross an hour, and the three-star plans five sections that use all three tunes."
  }
}
